\title{Temporal Measurement Drift in LLM Benchmarks:\\A Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models on MMLU}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%---------- ABSTRACT ----------
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 70\% on MMLU should reflect the same latent ability whether measured in 2023 or 2024. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis---a standard psychometric tool---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items). After conservative controls for permutation null rates and ability matching, we find that 20--28\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C) between temporal model cohorts, far exceeding the $<$0.2\% random-split baseline. This instability is item-specific rather than domain-driven (domain improvement explains $\leq$2.4 percentage points) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\rho \approx 0.994$ after removing all flagged items). We show that web-overlap contamination labels---the field's standard ground truth---measure global exposure rather than differential contamination, explaining why DIF enrichment against these labels is $\approx$1.0. As our primary methodological contribution, we propose a semi-synthetic validation framework: injecting controlled contamination into real response matrices with cross-subject ability matching, which eliminates false-positive inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$) while achieving meaningful detection power ($\Delta\text{TPR} = 0.37$--$0.42$ at exploitation rate $p{=}0.5$). The framework generalizes across benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA). We release the framework as reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector.
\end{abstract}

%---------- 1. INTRODUCTION ----------
\section{Introduction}

When we report that ``Model B scores 5 points higher than Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores measure the same construct on the same measurement scale. In educational testing, this assumption is routinely verified through Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. If many items function differently across groups, cross-group score comparisons become unreliable---not because the test is ``bad,'' but because the measurement instrument behaves differently for different populations.

LLM benchmarks face a version of this problem that human testing largely does not: the examinee population changes rapidly and systematically. Models released in 2024 differ from those released in 2023 not just in ability but potentially in training data composition, optimization procedures, and architectural choices. If benchmark items function differently across these temporal cohorts, then temporal score comparisons are confounded.

We apply MH-DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix---5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items---constituting the largest psychometric DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our analysis reveals that after conservative controls (permutation null subtraction, ability matching), approximately 20--28\% of MMLU items exhibit large differential functioning between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. Critically, this instability does not threaten benchmark validity: removing all flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\rho \approx 0.994$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.

A key insight motivating this work is that existing contamination detection methods---ConStat perturbation testing, generation variance analysis, Min-K\% likelihood methods---are typically validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels. These labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible (global exposure), not whether it functions differently across model cohorts (differential contamination). DIF enrichment $\approx 1.0$ against such labels is therefore expected, not a failure of DIF.

To address the validation gap, we contribute a \textbf{semi-synthetic validation framework} as our primary methodological contribution. By injecting known contamination patterns into real response matrices and using cross-subject ability matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$), we provide controlled ground truth for evaluating any item-level detector. The framework generalizes across benchmarks (confirmed on MMLU, GSM8K, and four additional METABENCH benchmarks).

\textbf{Key framing}: Instability $\neq$ invalidity. DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed across model generations. This is a \emph{diagnostic signal}---revealing which capability dimensions are shifting---not a quality verdict on the benchmark.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Empirical characterization}: 20--28\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF after conservative controls. The phenomenon generalizes across six METABENCH benchmarks (14.8--31.3\% C-rate range), is item-specific (domain improvement explains $\leq$7.6\%), and does not compromise ranking validity ($\rho > 0.993$).
    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors. We provide empirical evidence (enrichment $\approx 1.0$) and concrete failure-case analysis for ConStat and generation-variance methods.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework} (primary): $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$). Reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector. Demonstrated on two benchmarks.
\end{enumerate}

%---------- 2. RELATED WORK ----------
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}
Existing approaches to detecting benchmark contamination in LLMs fall into several categories. \emph{N-gram and web-overlap methods} identify items appearing verbatim in training corpora or web crawls, producing binary exposure labels. \emph{Perturbation-based methods} (e.g., ConStat) compare model performance on original vs.\ semantically equivalent reformulations, flagging items where performance drops significantly after paraphrasing. \emph{Generation variance methods} (e.g., DVD) detect contamination through reduced output variance on memorized items. \emph{Likelihood-based methods} (Min-K\%) flag items with unusually high token-level likelihoods. All these approaches share a common limitation: they are validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels that measure global exposure rather than differential item functioning.

\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}
Mantel--Haenszel DIF analysis, introduced by Holland and Thayer (1988) building on the Mantel--Haenszel statistic (1959), is the standard method for detecting items that function differently across demographic groups in high-stakes testing. The Educational Testing Service (ETS) classifies items into categories A (negligible DIF), B (moderate), and C (large, $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$). DIF analysis is routinely used in test development and has been applied in various educational contexts but, to our knowledge, not previously at the scale of LLM evaluation.

\paragraph{LLM benchmark analysis.}
Recent work has raised concerns about benchmark reliability from various angles: score saturation, data contamination, and evaluation methodology. Growing Pains and related work use anchor-item designs for efficient evaluation. Our DIF analysis complements these efforts by identifying \emph{which} items' measurement properties are changing, providing diagnostic information that anchor-item approaches can directly use.

%---------- 3. METHOD ----------
\section{Method}

\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}

Given a binary item response matrix $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^{N \times I}$ where $N$ models respond to $I$ items, we partition models into reference ($R$) and focal ($F$) groups and condition on total score strata $k = 1, \ldots, K$. For each item $i$, the MH odds ratio is:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH},i} = \frac{\sum_k A_{ik} D_{ik} / T_{ik}}{\sum_k B_{ik} C_{ik} / T_{ik}}
\end{equation}
where $A_{ik}, B_{ik}, C_{ik}, D_{ik}$ are the entries of the $2 \times 2$ table (group $\times$ correctness) at stratum $k$, and $T_{ik}$ is the stratum total. The MH delta statistic $\Delta_{\text{MH},i} = -2.35 \ln(\hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH},i})$ is classified per ETS guidelines: $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (Category A, negligible), $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (Category B, moderate), $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ (Category C, large).

\paragraph{Temporal cohort definition.}
We define reference group as models released in 2023 ($n_R = 1{,}080$) and focal group as models released in 2024-H1 ($n_F = 807$). The matching variable is total score across all MMLU items.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}

To evaluate DIF detection power under known ground truth, we inject controlled contamination into real response matrices:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item Select $n_{\text{inj}}$ clean items and designate them as ``contaminated.''
    \item For each focal-group model and each injected item answered incorrectly, flip the response to correct with probability $p$ (exploitation rate).
    \item Run MH-DIF on the modified matrix and compute $\Delta\text{TPR} = \text{TPR}_{\text{injected}} - \text{TPR}_{\text{baseline}}$ and $\Delta\text{FPR} = \text{FPR}_{\text{post}} - \text{FPR}_{\text{pre}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}
The critical methodological innovation is using total score on \emph{other} MMLU subjects (excluding the subject containing the injected items) as the MH matching variable. This eliminates matching variable contamination: injected performance gains do not inflate the conditioning variable. We demonstrate that within-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.19, while cross-subject matching maintains $\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$.

%---------- 4. EXPERIMENTS ----------
\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:temporal_dif}

We apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohort grouping (2023 vs.\ 2024-H1).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU. Raw C\% = 31.3\%; after permutation null subtraction and ability matching, the effective estimate is $\sim$24.5\%.}
\label{tab:temporal_dif}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrc@{}}
\toprule
Cohort Def. & $n_R$ & $n_F$ & Raw C\% & Perm.\ Null & Eff.\ C\% \\
\midrule
Temporal 2023 vs 2024 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 5.2 & 24.5 \\
Ability quintile & 2,100 & 2,100 & 2.9 & 0.3 & 2.6 \\
Random split (sanity) & 950 & 950 & 0.18 & --- & $\sim$0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Within-family confound control. Ability matching substantially reduces C\% for high-ability-gap pairs (Llama-2 vs 3: $-$33pp) but has minimal effect on the temporal cohort ($-$2.4pp), indicating that temporal DIF is not primarily an ability confound.}
\label{tab:within_family}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Family Pair & Raw C\% & Cohen's $d$ & Matched C\% & Interpretation \\
\midrule
Llama-2 vs Llama-3 & 61.3 & $-$0.99 & 28.3 & Ability: 33pp \\
Mistral vs Mixtral & 38.7 & $-$0.45 & $\sim$30 & Moderate ability \\
Temporal 2023 vs 2024 & 31.3 & $-$0.17 & 28.9 & Ability: 2.4pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:temporal_dif} presents the core temporal DIF result. Raw C\% is 31.3\%, but conservative interpretation requires accounting for two sources of non-DIF signal. First, the permutation null (\S\ref{sec:permutation}) establishes a 5.2\% baseline C-rate from random temporal label assignment. Second, ability matching reduces C\% by 2.4 percentage points ($d = -0.17$ between cohorts; Table~\ref{tab:within_family}). The effective estimate after all controls is approximately 24.5\%, which remains far above the random-split sanity check (0.18\%).

Domain-specific C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% across all 57 MMLU subjects (unweighted mean 30.6\%), with a modest ecological correlation between subject-level accuracy improvement and C\% ($\rho = 0.31$, $p = 0.02$). However, item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\% C-rate (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$), confirming that the instability is predominantly item-specific.

\subsection{Permutation Null Distribution}
\label{sec:permutation}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Permutation null distribution (1,000 permutations of temporal labels).}
\label{tab:permutation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
\toprule
Statistic & Value \\
\midrule
Null mean C\% & 5.2 \\
Null SD & 0.8 \\
Null 95th percentile & 6.5 \\
Null 99th percentile & 7.3 \\
Observed C\% & 31.3 \\
Excess over null mean & 26.1 \\
$z$-score & 32.6 \\
Permutation $p$ & $<$0.001 \\
Within-cohort random C\% & 0.18 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

To establish that the observed 31.3\% C-rate is not a statistical artifact of the temporal label assignment, we conduct a 1,000-iteration permutation test, randomly reassigning temporal labels while preserving model-level dependencies. Table~\ref{tab:permutation} shows the null C-rate distribution has mean 5.2\% (SD = 0.8). The observed rate exceeds the null by $z = 32.6$ ($p < 0.001$).

The 5.2\% null rate is non-trivial---it is 29$\times$ higher than the within-cohort random split (0.18\%)---and indicates that temporal labels carry some non-DIF signal beyond differential item functioning. This likely reflects composite confounds: cohort composition shifts, training data vintage correlations, and RLHF protocol evolution. The excess C-rate of $\sim$26\% above the permutation null represents genuine temporal measurement drift.

\subsection{Architecture Stratification}
\label{sec:architecture}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Architecture stratification. METABENCH MMLU is 99.9\% decoder-only; restricting to this subpopulation has negligible effect on DIF or IRT fit.}
\label{tab:architecture}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Population & $N$ & C\% & RMSEA & CFI \\
\midrule
Full & 5,227 & 31.3 & 0.082 & 0.72 \\
Decoder-only & 5,222 & 31.2 & 0.081 & 0.72 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A potential concern is that architectural heterogeneity (encoder-decoder vs.\ decoder-only models) could inflate spurious DIF. Table~\ref{tab:architecture} shows this is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU contains only 5 non-decoder-only models out of 5,227 (99.9\% decoder-only). Restricting to the homogeneous decoder-only subpopulation yields C\% = 31.2\% (Jaccard = 0.998 vs.\ full population).

The IRT fit statistics (RMSEA = 0.081, CFI = 0.72) do not improve under architectural homogeneity, confirming that model misfit ($|L_z| > 2$ for 97.8\% of models) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects forced into a unidimensional model), not population heterogeneity. We discuss implications for IRT model specification in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Temporal DIF}
\label{sec:multi_bench}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks (2023 vs.\ 2024 cohorts). All benchmarks show C-rates far exceeding random controls.}
\label{tab:multi_bench}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Benchmark & Items & $n_R$ & $n_F$ & C\% & Random C\% \\
\midrule
MMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\
WinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 26.1 & 0.35 \\
TruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 25.3 & 0.40 \\
ARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 22.5 & 0.50 \\
GSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 15.2 & 0.25 \\
HellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 14.8 & 0.22 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

To test whether temporal measurement drift is specific to MMLU or a general phenomenon, we apply the same MH-DIF analysis to five additional METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\ref{tab:multi_bench}). C-rates range from 14.8\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\% (MMLU), with all six benchmarks showing enrichment ratios $\geq 27\times$ over random controls. Four of six benchmarks exceed 20\% C-rate. The variation reflects task characteristics: HellaSwag's homogeneous commonsense reasoning items show less temporal drift than MMLU's heterogeneous 57-subject structure. Temporal measurement drift is a general phenomenon across LLM benchmarks, with benchmark-specific magnitude.

\subsection{Comparison with Perturbation-Based Detection (ConStat)}
\label{sec:constat}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{ConStat-style perturbation detection on MMLU by model category. Performance degrades sharply for reasoning-RL models (AUROC $\approx$ chance). Overlap with DIF flags is minimal (Jaccard = 0.13).}
\label{tab:constat}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Category & $n$ & AUROC & FDR $q{=}.05$ Flags & DIF Jaccard \\
\midrule
SFT only & 15 & 0.67 & 16.3\% & 0.14 \\
Alignment RL & 12 & 0.64 & 13.8\% & 0.12 \\
Reasoning RL & 8 & 0.51 & 8.1\% & 0.09 \\
All & 35 & 0.63 & 14.2\% & 0.13 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 220 MMLU items paraphrased via LLM (semantic similarity = 0.91, human quality rating = 3.8/5). Table~\ref{tab:constat} shows AUROC = 0.67 for SFT models but drops to 0.51 (near chance) for reasoning-RL models, consistent with the concern that reasoning-trained models do not rely on verbatim memorization. The low ConStat--DIF overlap (Jaccard = 0.13, $\phi = 0.08$) empirically confirms that these methods measure different constructs: ConStat detects individual-model memorization of surface forms, while DIF detects cohort-level differential functioning. Web-overlap label concordance is only 0.22, indicating that even ConStat's own signals diverge substantially from the web-overlap ground truth the field relies on.

\subsection{Generation Variance Detection (DVD)}
\label{sec:dvd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{DVD generation variance detection on MMLU (12 models, 50 responses/item, $T{=}0.8$). AUROC near chance for variant contamination.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Contamination Type & AUROC & Bimodal \% & SNR \\
\midrule
Verbatim & 0.62 & 9.2 & 1.5 \\
Variant & 0.55 & 6.1 & 1.1 \\
Overall & 0.58 & 7.8 & 1.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:dvd} presents generation-variance detection results on MMLU. Overall AUROC = 0.58 barely exceeds chance, with a signal-to-noise ratio of only 1.3. Performance is temperature-sensitive (AUROC range 0.52--0.64). The fundamental limitation is structural: MMLU's 4-choice MCQ format constrains generation variance to logit distributions over 4 options, providing insufficient signal space. Bimodal items (7.8\%) overlap minimally with DIF flags (Jaccard = 0.11), further confirming that generation variance captures a different phenomenon---likely item ambiguity rather than differential contamination. Together with the ConStat results (\S\ref{sec:constat}), these findings demonstrate that existing per-model detectors each have structural limitations on MCQ benchmarks, and that low overlap with DIF reflects genuinely different measurement constructs rather than DIF failure.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}
\label{sec:semi_synthetic}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power using cross-subject matching on MMLU. $\Delta$TPR increases monotonically with exploitation rate while $\Delta$FPR remains $<$0.001.}
\label{tab:semi_synthetic}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Expl.\ Rate $p$ & TPR & FPR & $\Delta$TPR & $\Delta$FPR \\
\midrule
0.0 (baseline) & 0.31 & 0.31 & 0.00 & 0.000 \\
0.3 & 0.45 & 0.31 & 0.14 & 0.001 \\
0.5 & 0.68 & 0.31 & 0.37 & 0.001 \\
0.7 & 0.88 & 0.31 & 0.57 & 0.001 \\
1.0 & 0.97 & 0.31 & 0.66 & 0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Within-subject vs.\ cross-subject matching. Within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation (+0.19) because the matching variable is contaminated; cross-subject matching eliminates this ($\Delta$FPR = 0.001).}
\label{tab:matching}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Matching Method & FPR (baseline) & FPR ($p{=}1.0$) & $\Delta$FPR \\
\midrule
Within-subject & 0.31 & 0.50 & 0.19 \\
Cross-subject & 0.31 & 0.311 & 0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Tables~\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}--\ref{tab:matching} present the core results of our semi-synthetic validation framework on MMLU. We inject known contamination into 150 items within a 500-item clean set, varying the exploitation rate $p$ (probability that each focal-group model's incorrect response is flipped to correct on injected items).

$\Delta$TPR = 0.37 at $p = 0.5$ (Table~\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}) demonstrates meaningful detection power at moderate contamination levels. The dose-response relationship is monotonic, confirming that DIF responds to the injected signal as intended. The elevated baseline FPR (0.31) reflects genuine pre-existing DIF in the MMLU matrix, not a methodological artifact.

The key methodological result is in Table~\ref{tab:matching}: cross-subject matching (using total score on other MMLU subjects as the conditioning variable) maintains $\Delta\text{FPR} = 0.001$ across all exploitation rates, while within-subject matching inflates FPR by 0.19 at $p = 1.0$. This confirms that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable when injected items contribute to the matching score---a subtle but critical design consideration for any DIF-based contamination analysis.

Injection prevalence sensitivity analysis shows $\Delta$TPR is robust: 0.28 at 5\% prevalence, rising to 0.40 at 30\% prevalence (at $p = 0.5$). Exploitation rate sensitivity shows detection power ranges from $\Delta$TPR = 0.12 at $p = 0.4$ to 0.66 at $p = 1.0$.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic $\Delta$TPR = 0.42 at $p{=}0.5$ confirms framework portability.}
\label{tab:gsm8k}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Metric & Value & 95\% CI lower & 95\% CI upper \\
\midrule
Temporal C\% & 15.2 & 12.8 & 17.9 \\
Semi-syn.\ $\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & 0.42 & 0.31 & 0.54 \\
GSM1K Pearson $r$ & $-$0.35 & $-$0.62 & $-$0.02 \\
GSM1K Spearman $\rho$ & $-$0.30 & $-$0.59 & 0.05 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We replicate our analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH). Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k} shows temporal C\% = 15.2\% (95\% CI: 12.8--17.9\%), lower than MMLU's 31.3\%, consistent with GSM8K being a single-domain benchmark with less structural heterogeneity. The semi-synthetic framework achieves $\Delta$TPR = 0.42 at $p = 0.5$, slightly higher than MMLU's 0.37, confirming cross-benchmark portability.

As an \emph{exploratory} analysis, we correlate DIF C-flags with GSM1K contamination gaps ($N = 33$ matched models). The Pearson correlation ($r = -0.35$, $p = 0.045$) is nominally significant but the CI is wide [$-$0.62, $-$0.02]; Spearman is non-significant ($\rho = -0.30$, $p = 0.09$). The negative direction (opposite to a naive contamination hypothesis) and small sample size preclude strong conclusions; we report this for transparency but do not build claims on it.

\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}
\label{sec:degradation}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ranking validity under progressive item replacement. Even removing 31\% of DIF-flagged items preserves $\rho > 0.993$.}
\label{tab:degradation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrr@{}}
\toprule
Replace \% & Spearman $\rho$ & SHR & Coverage \\
\midrule
5 & 0.9991 & 0.998 & 0.99 \\
10 & 0.9982 & 0.996 & 0.98 \\
20 & 0.9958 & 0.993 & 0.96 \\
31 & 0.9935 & 0.989 & 0.94 \\
50 & 0.9870 & 0.975 & 0.86 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:degradation} quantifies the downstream impact of temporal DIF on benchmark validity. Even at 31\% replacement (the observed C-rate), Spearman $\rho = 0.9935$ [0.991, 0.996] between full and reduced rankings, confirming massive measurement redundancy. Targeted DIF-item replacement ($\rho = 0.993$) causes slightly more disruption than random replacement ($\rho = 0.997$), indicating that DIF items carry marginally more ranking-relevant information than random items, but the difference is small. Construct coverage drops to 0.94 at 31\% removal---a 6\% loss that may matter in high-stakes contexts but does not fundamentally alter the measurement.

\subsection{Odds Ratio Regression Analysis}
\label{sec:odds_ratio}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Logistic regression models predicting DIF-C status. Even the best model (M4) explains only 7.3\% of DIF variance, confirming that instability is predominantly item-specific.}
\label{tab:odds_ratio}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Predictors & McF.\ $R^2$ & Nag.\ $R^2$ & Key OR \\
\midrule
M1 & Domain $\Delta$acc & .0007 & .0012 & 1.55/0.10 \\
M2 & M1 + item props & .042 & .071 & diff 0.78, disc 1.51 \\
M3 & Item $\Delta$acc & .0012 & .0021 & 1.08/0.10 \\
M4 & M3 + item props & .043 & .073 & $\Delta$acc 0.92 (rev.) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:odds_ratio} presents logistic regression models predicting DIF-C classification from observable item and domain properties. Domain-level accuracy improvement alone (M1) yields McFadden $R^2 = 0.0007$---domain improvement explains essentially nothing at the item level. Even with item properties added (M2), total explained variance reaches only 7.3\% (Nagelkerke $R^2 = 0.073$). A Simpson's paradox emerges: the item accuracy change odds ratio flips from 1.55 (M1, positive) to 0.92 (M4, reversed) when item properties are controlled, indicating that difficulty and discrimination are confounders. We report M1 as the primary descriptive model and M4 as supplementary; 92.7\% of DIF variance remains unexplained by observable characteristics.

\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}
\label{sec:ift}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Item-Focused Trees (IFT) vs.\ MH-DIF comparison. Substantial overlap (Jaccard = 0.72) validates the MH binary cohort split; IFT additionally reveals nonlinear temporal patterns.}
\label{tab:ift}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Metric & IFT & MH-DIF \\
\midrule
Flagged \% & 28.5 & 31.3 \\
Unique flags \% & 5.2 & 7.8 \\
Overlap Jaccard & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.72} \\
Computational cost & 15$\times$ & 1$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

As a robustness check, we apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which use model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring a binary cohort split. Table~\ref{tab:ift} shows IFT flags 28.5\% of items (vs.\ MH's 31.3\%), with Jaccard overlap of 0.72. The automatically identified split points concentrate in 2023-Q4 to 2024-Q1, validating our temporal cohort definition. Among IFT-flagged items, 37\% show nonlinear temporal patterns (neither monotonic increase nor decrease), suggesting that measurement drift is not uniform across time but reflects specific model release waves. The 15$\times$ computational cost and added interpretive complexity make IFT better suited as a supplementary validation than a primary analysis tool.

%---------- 5. DISCUSSION ----------
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{IRT model misspecification bounds.}
A fundamental concern with our MH-DIF analysis is that the underlying unidimensional IRT model is misspecified for MMLU: 98\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, CFI = 0.72, and RMSEA = 0.082. This misfit is structural---MMLU's 57 subjects represent a highly multidimensional construct forced into a unidimensional framework. A bifactor pilot on 5 subjects (Appendix~\ref{app:bifactor}) shows RMSEA = 0.038 (acceptable) but CFI = 0.89 (below the 0.95 target), with the general factor explaining only 42\% of variance. The full 57-subject bifactor model fails to converge.

How much could misspecification affect our DIF findings? Three observations bound the impact: (1) architecture stratification (\S\ref{sec:architecture}) shows that eliminating population heterogeneity does not improve fit, confirming the source is item-side multidimensionality; (2) the convergence between ability-matched temporal C\% ($\sim$28.9\%) and within-family matched C\% ($\sim$28.3\%) suggests the DIF signal is robust to different ability-conditioning approaches; (3) IFT results (\S\ref{sec:ift}) using a non-IRT method show Jaccard = 0.72 overlap with MH-DIF, indicating that the core finding is not an artifact of the 1D IRT assumption. Nonetheless, we acknowledge that DIF effect sizes may change under multidimensional modeling---the \emph{direction} of DIF findings is likely robust, but \emph{magnitudes} should be interpreted with appropriate caution.

\paragraph{Implications for benchmark maintenance.}
Our findings suggest a practical audit protocol: periodic MH-DIF analysis on growing response matrices, with cross-subject matching for contamination-specific validation. The ``instability $\neq$ invalidity'' framing is critical---benchmark maintainers need not panic at high C-rates, but should use DIF as a diagnostic tool to understand which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations. Growing Pains-style anchor-item approaches can directly consume DIF flags: our analysis identifies which anchor items are degrading.

%---------- 6. LIMITATIONS ----------
\section{Limitations}

\paragraph{IRT misspecification.}
As discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}, unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU. A pilot bifactor analysis on 5 subjects (Appendix~\ref{app:bifactor}) shows suboptimal fit (CFI = 0.89). Future work should explore multidimensional IRT approaches, though computational feasibility at 12,508 items remains challenging.

\paragraph{RL robustness.}
Our analysis of DIF stability under reasoning-RL model inclusion is inconclusive (Appendix~\ref{app:rl_robustness}). With only $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models, DIF flag Jaccard = 0.75 [0.62, 0.87] when these models are added to the focal cohort. The confidence interval spans ``essentially stable'' to ``moderate shift,'' and TOST equivalence testing fails ($p = 0.18$). RL robustness remains an open question.

\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}
Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping at rate $p$) is a simplification of real contamination processes. Actual contamination may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or format-specific advantages not captured by binary flipping. The elevated baseline FPR (0.31) means our $\Delta$TPR estimates are conservative.

%---------- 7. CONCLUSION ----------
\section{Conclusion}

We applied Mantel--Haenszel DIF analysis to 5,227 models on MMLU and five additional benchmarks, finding that 20--28\% of MMLU items (after conservative controls) exhibit large temporal DIF---a general phenomenon across LLM benchmarks (14.8--31.3\% C-rate). This instability is item-specific, not domain-driven, and does not threaten ranking validity ($\rho > 0.993$). We demonstrated that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors and proposed a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$) as reusable infrastructure for the community.

