Simulating cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across 4 successive benchmark administrations (Table~\ref{tab:bayesian}), we find power at 3 administrations reaches 0.55 for medium effects ($\delta = 1.0$), below the 0.70 target. For large effects ($\delta \geq 1.4$), cumulative power reaches 0.82, but these items are already detected at 0.68 power in a single administration, limiting the marginal value. The approach is theoretically sound but does not provide sufficient practical improvement to justify the methodological complexity of maintaining longitudinal DIF databases. False positive rate remains controlled at 0.058.

