\title{Temporal Measurement Drift in LLM Benchmarks:\\A Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models on MMLU}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%---------- ABSTRACT ----------
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 70\% on MMLU should reflect the same latent ability whether measured in 2023 or 2024. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis---a standard psychometric tool---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items). After conservative controls for permutation null rates and ability matching, we find that 20--28\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C) between temporal model cohorts, far exceeding the $<$0.2\% random-split baseline. This instability is item-specific rather than domain-driven (domain improvement explains $\leq$2.4 percentage points) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\rho \approx 0.994$ after removing all flagged items). We show that web-overlap contamination labels---the field's standard ground truth---measure global exposure rather than differential contamination, explaining why DIF enrichment against these labels is $\approx$1.0. As our primary methodological contribution, we propose a semi-synthetic validation framework: injecting controlled contamination into real response matrices with cross-subject ability matching, which eliminates false-positive inflation ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$) while achieving meaningful detection power ($\Delta\text{TPR} = 0.37$--$0.42$ at exploitation rate $p{=}0.5$). The framework generalizes across benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA). We release the framework as reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector.
\end{abstract}

%---------- 1. INTRODUCTION ----------
\section{Introduction}

When we report that ``Model B scores 5 points higher than Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores measure the same construct on the same measurement scale. In educational testing, this assumption is routinely verified through Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. If many items function differently across groups, cross-group score comparisons become unreliable---not because the test is ``bad,'' but because the measurement instrument behaves differently for different populations.

LLM benchmarks face a version of this problem that human testing largely does not: the examinee population changes rapidly and systematically. Models released in 2024 differ from those released in 2023 not just in ability but potentially in training data composition, optimization procedures, and architectural choices. If benchmark items function differently across these temporal cohorts, then temporal score comparisons are confounded.

We apply MH-DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix---5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items---constituting the largest psychometric DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our analysis reveals that after conservative controls (permutation null subtraction, ability matching), approximately 20--28\% of MMLU items exhibit large differential functioning between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. Critically, this instability does not threaten benchmark validity: removing all flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\rho \approx 0.994$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.

A key insight motivating this work is that existing contamination detection methods---ConStat perturbation testing, generation variance analysis, Min-K\% likelihood methods---are typically validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels. These labels measure whether an item is publicly accessible (global exposure), not whether it functions differently across model cohorts (differential contamination). DIF enrichment $\approx 1.0$ against such labels is therefore expected, not a failure of DIF.

To address the validation gap, we contribute a \textbf{semi-synthetic validation framework} as our primary methodological contribution. By injecting known contamination patterns into real response matrices and using cross-subject ability matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$), we provide controlled ground truth for evaluating any item-level detector. The framework generalizes across benchmarks (confirmed on MMLU, GSM8K, and four additional METABENCH benchmarks).

\textbf{Key framing}: Instability $\neq$ invalidity. DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed across model generations. This is a \emph{diagnostic signal}---revealing which capability dimensions are shifting---not a quality verdict on the benchmark.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Empirical characterization}: 20--28\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF after conservative controls. The phenomenon generalizes across six METABENCH benchmarks (14.8--31.3\% C-rate range), is item-specific (domain improvement explains $\leq$7.6\%), and does not compromise ranking validity ($\rho > 0.993$).
    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors. We provide empirical evidence (enrichment $\approx 1.0$) and concrete failure-case analysis for ConStat and generation-variance methods.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework} (primary): $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$). Reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector. Demonstrated on two benchmarks.
\end{enumerate}

%---------- 2. RELATED WORK ----------
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}
Existing approaches to detecting benchmark contamination in LLMs fall into several categories. \emph{N-gram and web-overlap methods} identify items appearing verbatim in training corpora or web crawls, producing binary exposure labels. \emph{Perturbation-based methods} (e.g., ConStat) compare model performance on original vs.\ semantically equivalent reformulations, flagging items where performance drops significantly after paraphrasing. \emph{Generation variance methods} (e.g., DVD) detect contamination through reduced output variance on memorized items. \emph{Likelihood-based methods} (Min-K\%) flag items with unusually high token-level likelihoods. All these approaches share a common limitation: they are validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels that measure global exposure rather than differential item functioning.

\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}
Mantel--Haenszel DIF analysis, introduced by Holland and Thayer (1988) building on the Mantel--Haenszel statistic (1959), is the standard method for detecting items that function differently across demographic groups in high-stakes testing. The Educational Testing Service (ETS) classifies items into categories A (negligible DIF), B (moderate), and C (large, $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$). DIF analysis is routinely used in test development and has been applied in various educational contexts but, to our knowledge, not previously at the scale of LLM evaluation.

\paragraph{LLM benchmark analysis.}
Recent work has raised concerns about benchmark reliability from various angles: score saturation, data contamination, and evaluation methodology. Growing Pains and related work use anchor-item designs for efficient evaluation. Our DIF analysis complements these efforts by identifying \emph{which} items' measurement properties are changing, providing diagnostic information that anchor-item approaches can directly use.

%---------- 3. METHOD ----------
\section{Method}

\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}

Given a binary item response matrix $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^{N \times I}$ where $N$ models respond to $I$ items, we partition models into reference ($R$) and focal ($F$) groups and condition on total score strata $k = 1, \ldots, K$. For each item $i$, the MH odds ratio is:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH},i} = \frac{\sum_k A_{ik} D_{ik} / T_{ik}}{\sum_k B_{ik} C_{ik} / T_{ik}}
\end{equation}
where $A_{ik}, B_{ik}, C_{ik}, D_{ik}$ are the entries of the $2 \times 2$ table (group $\times$ correctness) at stratum $k$, and $T_{ik}$ is the stratum total. The MH delta statistic $\Delta_{\text{MH},i} = -2.35 \ln(\hat{\alpha}_{\text{MH},i})$ is classified per ETS guidelines: $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (Category A, negligible), $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (Category B, moderate), $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ (Category C, large).

\paragraph{Temporal cohort definition.}
We define reference group as models released in 2023 ($n_R = 1{,}080$) and focal group as models released in 2024-H1 ($n_F = 807$). The matching variable is total score across all MMLU items.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}

To evaluate DIF detection power under known ground truth, we inject controlled contamination into real response matrices:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item Select $n_{\text{inj}}$ clean items and designate them as ``contaminated.''
    \item For each focal-group model and each injected item answered incorrectly, flip the response to correct with probability $p$ (exploitation rate).
    \item Run MH-DIF on the modified matrix and compute $\Delta\text{TPR} = \text{TPR}_{\text{injected}} - \text{TPR}_{\text{baseline}}$ and $\Delta\text{FPR} = \text{FPR}_{\text{post}} - \text{FPR}_{\text{pre}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}
The critical methodological innovation is using total score on \emph{other} MMLU subjects (excluding the subject containing the injected items) as the MH matching variable. This eliminates matching variable contamination: injected performance gains do not inflate the conditioning variable. We demonstrate that within-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.19, while cross-subject matching maintains $\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$.

%---------- 4. EXPERIMENTS ----------
\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:temporal_dif}

We apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohort grouping (2023 vs.\ 2024-H1).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU. Raw C\% = 31.3\%; after permutation null subtraction and ability matching, the effective estimate is $\sim$24.5\%.}
\label{tab:temporal_dif}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrc@{}}
\toprule
Cohort Def. & $n_R$ & $n_F$ & Raw C\% & Perm.\ Null & Eff.\ C\% \\
\midrule
Temporal 2023 vs 2024 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 5.2 & 24.5 \\
Ability quintile & 2,100 & 2,100 & 2.9 & 0.3 & 2.6 \\
Random split (sanity) & 950 & 950 & 0.18 & --- & $\sim$0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Within-family confound control. Ability matching substantially reduces C\% for high-ability-gap pairs (Llama-2 vs 3: $-$33pp) but has minimal effect on the temporal cohort ($-$2.4pp), indicating that temporal DIF is not primarily an ability confound.}
\label{tab:within_family}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Family Pair & Raw C\% & Cohen's $d$ & Matched C\% & Interpretation \\
\midrule
Llama-2 vs Llama-3 & 61.3 & $-$0.99 & 28.3 & Ability: 33pp \\
Mistral vs Mixtral & 38.7 & $-$0.45 & $\sim$30 & Moderate ability \\
Temporal 2023 vs 2024 & 31.3 & $-$0.17 & 28.9 & Ability: 2.4pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:temporal_dif} presents the core temporal DIF result. Raw C\% is 31.3\%, but conservative interpretation requires accounting for two sources of non-DIF signal. First, the permutation null (\S\ref{sec:permutation}) establishes a 5.2\% baseline C-rate from random temporal label assignment. Second, ability matching reduces C\% by 2.4 percentage points ($d = -0.17$ between cohorts; Table~\ref{tab:within_family}). The effective estimate after all controls is approximately 24.5\%, which remains far above the random-split sanity check (0.18\%).

Domain-specific C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% across all 57 MMLU subjects (unweighted mean 30.6\%), with a modest ecological correlation between subject-level accuracy improvement and C\% ($\rho = 0.31$, $p = 0.02$). However, item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\% C-rate (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$), confirming that the instability is predominantly item-specific.

\subsection{Permutation Null Distribution}
\label{sec:permutation}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Permutation null distribution (1,000 permutations of temporal labels).}
\label{tab:permutation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
\toprule
Statistic & Value \\
\midrule
Null mean C\% & 5.2 \\
Null SD & 0.8 \\
Null 95th percentile & 6.5 \\
Null 99th percentile & 7.3 \\
Observed C\% & 31.3 \\
Excess over null mean & 26.1 \\
$z$-score & 32.6 \\
Permutation $p$ & $<$0.001 \\
Within-cohort random C\% & 0.18 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

To establish that the observed 31.3\% C-rate is not a statistical artifact of the temporal label assignment, we conduct a 1,000-iteration permutation test, randomly reassigning temporal labels while preserving model-level dependencies. Table~\ref{tab:permutation} shows the null C-rate distribution has mean 5.2\% (SD = 0.8). The observed rate exceeds the null by $z = 32.6$ ($p < 0.001$).

The 5.2\% null rate is non-trivial---it is 29$\times$ higher than the within-cohort random split (0.18\%)---and indicates that temporal labels carry some non-DIF signal beyond differential item functioning. This likely reflects composite confounds: cohort composition shifts, training data vintage correlations, and RLHF protocol evolution. The excess C-rate of $\sim$26\% above the permutation null represents genuine temporal measurement drift.

\subsection{Architecture Stratification}
\label{sec:architecture}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Architecture stratification. METABENCH MMLU is 99.9\% decoder-only; restricting to this subpopulation has negligible effect on DIF or IRT fit.}
\label{tab:architecture}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Population & $N$ & C\% & RMSEA & CFI \\
\midrule
Full & 5,227 & 31.3 & 0.082 & 0.72 \\
Decoder-only & 5,222 & 31.2 & 0.081 & 0.72 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A potential concern is that architectural heterogeneity (encoder-decoder vs.\ decoder-only models) could inflate spurious DIF. Table~\ref{tab:architecture} shows this is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU contains only 5 non-decoder-only models out of 5,227 (99.9\% decoder-only). Restricting to the homogeneous decoder-only subpopulation yields C\% = 31.2\% (Jaccard = 0.998 vs.\ full population).

The IRT fit statistics (RMSEA = 0.081, CFI = 0.72) do not improve under architectural homogeneity, confirming that model misfit ($|L_z| > 2$ for 97.8\% of models) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects forced into a unidimensional model), not population heterogeneity. We discuss implications for IRT model specification in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Temporal DIF}
\label{sec:multi_bench}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks (2023 vs.\ 2024 cohorts). All benchmarks show C-rates far exceeding random controls.}
\label{tab:multi_bench}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Benchmark & Items & $n_R$ & $n_F$ & C\% & Random C\% \\
\midrule
MMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\
WinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 26.1 & 0.35 \\
TruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 25.3 & 0.40 \\
ARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 22.5 & 0.50 \\
GSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 15.2 & 0.25 \\
HellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 14.8 & 0.22 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

To test whether temporal measurement drift is specific to MMLU or a general phenomenon, we apply the same MH-DIF analysis to five additional METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\ref{tab:multi_bench}). C-rates range from 14.8\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\% (MMLU), with all six benchmarks showing enrichment ratios $\geq 27\times$ over random controls. Four of six benchmarks exceed 20\% C-rate. The variation reflects task characteristics: HellaSwag's homogeneous commonsense reasoning items show less temporal drift than MMLU's heterogeneous 57-subject structure. Temporal measurement drift is a general phenomenon across LLM benchmarks, with benchmark-specific magnitude.

