Tables~\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}--\ref{tab:matching} present the core results of our semi-synthetic validation framework on MMLU. We inject known contamination into 150 items within a 500-item clean set, varying the exploitation rate $p$ (probability that each focal-group model's incorrect response is flipped to correct on injected items).

$\Delta$TPR = 0.37 at $p = 0.5$ (Table~\ref{tab:semi_synthetic}) demonstrates meaningful detection power at moderate contamination levels. The dose-response relationship is monotonic, confirming that DIF responds to the injected signal as intended. The elevated baseline FPR (0.31) reflects genuine pre-existing DIF in the MMLU matrix, not a methodological artifact.

The key methodological result is in Table~\ref{tab:matching}: cross-subject matching (using total score on other MMLU subjects as the conditioning variable) maintains $\Delta\text{FPR} = 0.001$ across all exploitation rates, while within-subject matching inflates FPR by 0.19 at $p = 1.0$. This confirms that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable when injected items contribute to the matching score---a subtle but critical design consideration for any DIF-based contamination analysis.

Injection prevalence sensitivity analysis shows $\Delta$TPR is robust: 0.28 at 5\% prevalence, rising to 0.40 at 30\% prevalence (at $p = 0.5$). Exploitation rate sensitivity shows detection power ranges from $\Delta$TPR = 0.12 at $p = 0.4$ to 0.66 at $p = 1.0$.

