%---------- 5. DISCUSSION ----------
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{IRT model misspecification bounds.}
A fundamental concern with our MH-DIF analysis is that the underlying unidimensional IRT model is misspecified for MMLU: 98\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, CFI = 0.72, and RMSEA = 0.082. This misfit is structural---MMLU's 57 subjects represent a highly multidimensional construct forced into a unidimensional framework. A bifactor pilot on 5 subjects (Appendix~\ref{app:bifactor}) shows RMSEA = 0.038 (acceptable) but CFI = 0.89 (below the 0.95 target), with the general factor explaining only 42\% of variance. The full 57-subject bifactor model fails to converge.

How much could misspecification affect our DIF findings? Three observations bound the impact: (1) architecture stratification (\S\ref{sec:architecture}) shows that eliminating population heterogeneity does not improve fit, confirming the source is item-side multidimensionality; (2) the convergence between ability-matched temporal C\% ($\sim$28.9\%) and within-family matched C\% ($\sim$28.3\%) suggests the DIF signal is robust to different ability-conditioning approaches; (3) IFT results (\S\ref{sec:ift}) using a non-IRT method show Jaccard = 0.72 overlap with MH-DIF, indicating that the core finding is not an artifact of the 1D IRT assumption. Nonetheless, we acknowledge that DIF effect sizes may change under multidimensional modeling---the \emph{direction} of DIF findings is likely robust, but \emph{magnitudes} should be interpreted with appropriate caution.

\paragraph{Implications for benchmark maintenance.}
Our findings suggest a practical audit protocol: periodic MH-DIF analysis on growing response matrices, with cross-subject matching for contamination-specific validation. The ``instability $\neq$ invalidity'' framing is critical---benchmark maintainers need not panic at high C-rates, but should use DIF as a diagnostic tool to understand which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations. Growing Pains-style anchor-item approaches can directly consume DIF flags: our analysis identifies which anchor items are degrading.

%---------- 6. LIMITATIONS ----------
\section{Limitations}

\paragraph{IRT misspecification.}
As discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}, unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU. A pilot bifactor analysis on 5 subjects (Appendix~\ref{app:bifactor}) shows suboptimal fit (CFI = 0.89). Future work should explore multidimensional IRT approaches, though computational feasibility at 12,508 items remains challenging.

\paragraph{RL robustness.}
Our analysis of DIF stability under reasoning-RL model inclusion is inconclusive (Appendix~\ref{app:rl_robustness}). With only $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models, DIF flag Jaccard = 0.75 [0.62, 0.87] when these models are added to the focal cohort. The confidence interval spans ``essentially stable'' to ``moderate shift,'' and TOST equivalence testing fails ($p = 0.18$). RL robustness remains an open question.

\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}
Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping at rate $p$) is a simplification of real contamination processes. Actual contamination may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or format-specific advantages not captured by binary flipping. The elevated baseline FPR (0.31) means our $\Delta$TPR estimates are conservative.

%---------- 7. CONCLUSION ----------
\section{Conclusion}

We applied Mantel--Haenszel DIF analysis to 5,227 models on MMLU and five additional benchmarks, finding that 20--28\% of MMLU items (after conservative controls) exhibit large temporal DIF---a general phenomenon across LLM benchmarks (14.8--31.3\% C-rate). This instability is item-specific, not domain-driven, and does not threaten ranking validity ($\rho > 0.993$). We demonstrated that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors and proposed a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta\text{FPR} < 0.001$) as reusable infrastructure for the community.

